// The cryptohash package: SHA-256, SHA-512 and SHA-1 (FIPS 180-4), HMAC over
// each (RFC 2104, FIPS 198-1), and a constant-time comparison for MACs.
// Written in Cone, with no C library and no call to the OS.
//
//   mod digest;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import cryptohash;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     imm d = cryptohash.sha256("abc");            // an Array[u8, 32] of the digest
//     printInt(i64.from(d[0]));                   // 186 (0xba, the first byte of ba7816bf...)
//
//     mut h = cryptohash.Sha256.make();           // streaming: the state is a value
//     h.update("a");
//     h.update("bc");
//     imm same = h.finish();                      // the same 32 bytes as d
//
//     imm tag = cryptohash.hmacSha256("a key", "a message");    // an Array[u8, 32]
//     mut mac = cryptohash.HmacSha256.make("a key");
//     mac.update("a message");
//     printInt(if mac.verify(&tag) { 1i64; } else { 0i64; });   // 1: compared in constant time
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// What is here, by name:
//
//   Sha256, Sha512, Sha1       streaming hashers: 'make', 'update' (also named
//                              'feed', which is what package streams' Digester
//                              asks for), 'finish', 'copyOf', 'reset', 'length'
//   sha256, sha512, sha1       one call for a whole message
//   HmacSha256, HmacSha512,    streaming MACs: 'make(key)', 'update', 'finish',
//   HmacSha1                   'verify(tag)', 'copyOf'
//   hmacSha256, hmacSha512,    one call for a key and a whole message
//   hmacSha1
//   constantTimeEq             equal strings, compared in constant time
//
// Digests and tags are fixed arrays: 'Array[u8, 32]' for SHA-256 and its HMAC,
// 'Array[u8, 64]' for SHA-512 and its HMAC, 'Array[u8, 20]' for SHA-1 and its
// HMAC. A message, key or tag is anything that lends a byte slice: a string
// literal, a String's 'view()', a List[u8]'s 'view()', a fixed array's '&'.
//
// SHA-1 IS LEGACY. SHA-1 collisions can be made (SHAttered, 2017; a
// chosen-prefix collision, 2019 and 2020), so it must not be used for a new
// security purpose: not for signatures, certificates, or telling files apart
// against an attacker. It is here for formats that name it (git object names,
// the WebSocket handshake, old protocols and the RFC 2202 vectors). Use
// SHA-256 or SHA-512 for anything new. HMAC-SHA-1 is not broken by collisions,
// but is for the same old uses.
//
// These are not password hashes. A password needs a slow, memory-hard function
// (Argon2, scrypt, bcrypt), which this package does not have; a fast hash of a
// password is a weak way to keep one. It has no key derivation (HKDF, PBKDF2)
// either; both are short to write over HMAC.
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says
// what was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - Written from FIPS 180-4 in plain integer arithmetic (additions that wrap,
//   rotates, shifts, xor, and, or): the algorithms' own operations, so the
//   same bytes come out on every machine and every byte order. The hasher
//   reads its input a byte at a time and builds each word big-endian itself.
//   Left out: the SIMD and hardware paths (the x86 SHA extensions, ARM's
//   crypto instructions, AVX2 multi-buffer), which Cone cannot spell, and
//   which would make a second code path to test. Speed is measured, not
//   chased: examples/throughput.cone prints it (about 225 MB/s for SHA-256,
//   335 for SHA-512 and SHA-1, and 255 for HMAC-SHA-256 in a release build on
//   the author's x86-64 laptop, bounds checks on).
// - The state is a value. A hasher is a struct of its chaining words, one
//   partly filled block, the fill and the byte count, with no global and no
//   thread-local behind it; make one per use, hand it on as '&mut Sha256', and
//   '&mut' keeps two threads from one. 'copyOf' forks a hash in progress (to
//   hash a common prefix once), and 'finish' takes '&' and works on a copy, so
//   a hasher can be asked for its digest and fed more afterwards (as Go's
//   'Sum' and Python's 'digest' do). 'reset' empties it.
// - The round constants are locals. SHA-256 needs 64 words and SHA-512 80; a
//   module-level table would be a global, and globals hold. Each compression
//   function builds its table as a local array on entry, and takes all the
//   blocks of one 'update' call per entry, so a large update pays for the
//   table once and a one-byte update pays for it on each full block only.
//   Left out: a function that works each constant out on demand (it needs
//   cube roots of primes to 64 bits of fraction), and a table held in each
//   hasher (it would copy 256 or 640 bytes with every 'copyOf').
// - The message length is a u64 count of bytes. SHA-256 and SHA-1 count it in
//   bits in 64, so are limited to 2^61 bytes by the standard; SHA-512's
//   standard length is 128 bits and here is the 64-bit byte count widened, so a
//   message past 2^61 bytes (two exabytes) is not hashed correctly. A
//   hasher cannot be fed that much in a lifetime.
// - HMAC follows RFC 2104: a key longer than the hash's block (64 bytes, 128
//   for SHA-512) is hashed first, a shorter one padded with zeros; the inner
//   and outer pads (0x36 and 0x5c) are fed to two hashers once, in 'make', and
//   kept, so a MAC costs its message plus two compressions however many times
//   it is asked. An empty key is allowed (RFC 2104 does not forbid it). 'verify'
//   answers false for a tag of the wrong length: a truncated tag is not
//   accepted, so a protocol that sends one compares the prefix it sent
//   itself. Left out: HMAC with other hashes, KMAC, and a one-shot that takes a
//   truncation.
// - The comparison ('constantTimeEq', which 'verify' calls) is written without
//   a branch or an index that depends on the data: it xors each pair of bytes,
//   or-s the results into one byte and tests that byte once, at the end. See
//   the function for what an optimiser could still do to it.
// - Key and state memory is not wiped after use. Cone has no way to say that
//   a store must not be removed, so a wipe could be dropped by the optimiser
//   and would promise what it cannot keep. A caller who must scrub holds its
//   key in memory it overwrites itself.
// - No global and no thread-local. The tests compare against NIST's published
//   examples and RFC test vectors and against Python's hashlib as an oracle,
//   which the package never calls.
//
// What the rest of the tree already has, and is not moved onto this package:
// core's 'Hasher' is wyhash, a fast non-cryptographic hash for hash tables,
// seeded from conestd's 'hash.cone' (the OS's entropy); 'tls' asks OpenSSL's
// EVP_Digest for the SHA-256 of a certificate's public key (a pin), since
// OpenSSL is TLS's only C library; Congo's Python tools hash with Python's
// hashlib. No SHA or HMAC was in conestd or core, and no caller is migrated.

mod cryptohash;
